src/babylon/proto/gen/babylon.btccheckpoint.v1.rs:2:/// Consider we have a Merkle tree with following structure:
src/babylon/proto/gen/babylon.btccheckpoint.v1.rs:3:///             ROOT
src/babylon/proto/gen/babylon.btccheckpoint.v1.rs:4:///            /    \
src/babylon/proto/gen/babylon.btccheckpoint.v1.rs:5:///       H1234      H5555
src/babylon/proto/gen/babylon.btccheckpoint.v1.rs:6:///      /     \       \
src/babylon/proto/gen/babylon.btccheckpoint.v1.rs:7:///    H12     H34      H55
src/babylon/proto/gen/babylon.btccheckpoint.v1.rs:8:///   /  \    /  \     /
src/babylon/proto/gen/babylon.btccheckpoint.v1.rs:9:/// H1  H2  H3  H4  H5
src/babylon/proto/gen/babylon.btccheckpoint.v1.rs:10:/// L1  L2  L3  L4  L5
src/babylon/proto/gen/babylon.btccheckpoint.v1.rs:11:/// To prove L3 was part of ROOT we need:
src/babylon/proto/gen/babylon.btccheckpoint.v1.rs:12:/// - btc_transaction_index = 2 which in binary is 010
src/babylon/proto/gen/babylon.btccheckpoint.v1.rs:13:/// (where 0 means going left, 1 means going right in the tree)
src/babylon/proto/gen/babylon.btccheckpoint.v1.rs:14:/// - merkle_nodes we'd have H4 || H12 || H5555
src/babylon/proto/gen/babylon.btccheckpoint.v1.rs:15:/// By looking at 010 we would know that H4 is a right sibling,
src/babylon/proto/gen/babylon.btccheckpoint.v1.rs:16:/// H12 is left, H5555 is right again.
