src/babylon/proto/gen/babylon.zoneconcierge.v1.rs:56:/// Forks is a list of non-canonical `IndexedHeader`s at the same height.
src/babylon/proto/gen/babylon.zoneconcierge.v1.rs:57:/// For example, assuming the following blockchain
src/babylon/proto/gen/babylon.zoneconcierge.v1.rs:58:/// ```
src/babylon/proto/gen/babylon.zoneconcierge.v1.rs:59:/// A <- B <- C <- D <- E
src/babylon/proto/gen/babylon.zoneconcierge.v1.rs:60:///             \ -- D1
src/babylon/proto/gen/babylon.zoneconcierge.v1.rs:61:///             \ -- D2
src/babylon/proto/gen/babylon.zoneconcierge.v1.rs:62:/// ```
src/babylon/proto/gen/babylon.zoneconcierge.v1.rs:63:/// Then the fork will be {\[D1, D2\]} where each item is in struct `IndexedBlock`.
src/babylon/proto/gen/babylon.zoneconcierge.v1.rs:64:///
src/babylon/proto/gen/babylon.zoneconcierge.v1.rs:65:/// Note that each `IndexedHeader` in the fork should have a valid quorum
src/babylon/proto/gen/babylon.zoneconcierge.v1.rs:66:/// certificate. Such forks exist since Babylon considers CZs might have
src/babylon/proto/gen/babylon.zoneconcierge.v1.rs:67:/// dishonest majority. Also note that the IBC-Go implementation will only
src/babylon/proto/gen/babylon.zoneconcierge.v1.rs:68:/// consider the first header in a fork valid, since the subsequent headers
src/babylon/proto/gen/babylon.zoneconcierge.v1.rs:69:/// cannot be verified without knowing the validator set in the previous header.
